# Every quantity a layer files, as rows rather than columns. One row per layer per quantity, with
# the element's own name as the key.
#
# One kind of thing
#   The model treats these as one kind of thing. `asrt:Elimination` carries a `quantity`, and the
#   corpus files eliminations naming three of them (`demand`, `nameplate` and `draw`), because two
#   members naming one machine count the machine twice as well as the orders.
#   composition/fused.sqlc sums demand, nameplate and draw; this relation adds the three buffer
#   slacks, which no sum reads.
#
# A fusion could move a nameplate in silence
#   `merge-group-composition/compute-us` is a one-part fusion with no eliminations. If its composed
#   nameplate moved to five times the single part it is composed from, a composition rule reading
#   only demand would not see it. checks/one_part_fusion_alters_its_part reads this relation and
#   fires on that edit, and `examples/witnesses` makes it.
#
# Why five and not two
#   A layer's demand, its nameplate and its three buffer slacks are all quantities in the layer's
#   own unit that a composition has to carry or account for. The three slacks arrive from
#   `entries/slacks.sqlc` already one row each, and the buffer enum names them exactly as the
#   elements do.
#
# An unsized quantity has no row
#   An unsized quantity is missing from this relation rather than present with a NULL. A row here
#   is a number somebody filed; a caller that needs to know a slack was declined reads
#   entries/slacks.sqlc, where the typed reason lives.
#
# The five names are a type, and not the fusion's
#   `public.layer_quantity` declares them, so a caller that misspells one fails when its query is
#   parsed instead of quietly matching nothing. A string nothing checks, such as a
#   `quantity = 'nameplate'` in units/conversions.sqlc, would empty the whole conversion graph in
#   silence when misspelt. `pm.summed_quantity` is the other set, the three a fusion sums, and the
#   two agree on `demand` and `nameplate` and on nothing else. A relation holding one against the
#   other therefore matches by name and casts both sides, to say that the name is all they share:
#   composition/carried.sqlc and algebra/carried.sqlc are the two that do.
#
# The slack arm builds its name, and the cast checks it
#   `pm.buffer || 'Slack'` makes the element's name out of the buffer's, so the three move together
#   with the enum they come from. A buffer this type does not name fails at the cast rather than
#   arriving downstream as a sixth quantity nobody declared.
#
# Probe
#   `examples/witnesses` moves `compute-us`'s composed nameplate to five times its part; run it,
#   and a composition rule must notice.
-- pm:Layer's own quantities: demand, nameplate and the three buffer slacks, keyed by element.
SELECT d.filing, d.layer, 'demand'::public.layer_quantity AS quantity,
       d.d_low AS low, d.d_mode AS mode, d.d_high AS high, d.d_unit AS unit
FROM (
    :compose(layers/demand.sqlc)
) d
UNION ALL
SELECT n.filing, n.layer, 'nameplate'::public.layer_quantity,
       n.n_low, n.n_mode, n.n_high, n.n_unit
FROM (
    :compose(layers/nameplate.sqlc)
) n
UNION ALL
SELECT s.filing, s.layer, (s.buffer || 'Slack')::public.layer_quantity,
       s.low, s.mode, s.high, s.unit
FROM (
    :compose(entries/slacks.sqlc)
) s
WHERE s.low IS NOT NULL
